{"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The weak-* policy-space argument may hide a measurability or continuity gap, especially in reducing arbitrary randomized histories to kernels depending only on public price prefixes. Sion may also be unnecessary but misapplied if compactness is not stated in the right topology.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","functional analysis audit","counterexample search"],"summary":"Prove or refute the finite-Theta, finite-grid minimax identity exactly as stated in the solver attempt. Focus on the reduction to public-prefix randomized kernels, construction of the compact convex policy space as products of L-infinity simplices dominated by prefix mixture measures, weak-* continuity of each L_theta, and the Sion/von Neumann minimax step.","title":"Audit finite-grid minimax identity","why_promising":"This is the solver attempt's central new claim. If it survives, it gives a precise necessary-and-sufficient finite-class characterization via least-favorable Bayes gap; if it fails, the failure point will sharply delimit what can be claimed."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"A classical theorem may require assumptions not present here, such as finite observation spaces, dominated experiments, compact action spaces with continuity, or nonnegative loss conventions. It may also prove only epsilon-minimax or value existence, not the exact displayed Psi_G formula.","suggested_lenses":["literature/theorem matching","proof strategy"],"summary":"Find the cleanest finite statistical decision theorem that implies the identity: finite parameter set, standard Borel observations/histories, finite action grid at each time, randomized decision kernels, bounded loss. Translate the delayed-market problem into that theorem and identify the Bayes risk term using the already accepted fixed-prior myopic lemma.","title":"Decision-theoretic shortcut","why_promising":"This could replace the solver's informal compactness proof with a standard minimax/least-favorable-prior result, making the final write-up shorter and more defensible."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"The bound may be T/K, 2T/K, or require separate one-sided inequalities depending on whether learner actions, comparator values, and Bayes envelopes are rounded consistently. Strict threshold nonattainment can break exact continuum statements if suprema are mishandled.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","edge-case audit","finite examples"],"summary":"Verify the grid-to-continuum approximation claimed by the solver. Prove both directions comparing R_[0,1] and R_GK using downward rounding, then check whether sup_pi Psi_GK approximates any continuum Bayes-gap object and whether positive-part upper-regret changes the statement.","title":"Continuum approximation check","why_promising":"Even if the finite-grid identity is true, the problem's original quote space is [0,1]. This direction isolates the discretization and sign-convention details that previously caused false claims."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Finite-tree computations may only test simplified models and could miss pathologies of general standard Borel histories. Conversely, a counterexample may expose a definition mismatch rather than invalidate the intended theorem.","suggested_lenses":["computational verification","counterexample search"],"summary":"Build exact finite observation-tree instances and compute the minimax value by linear programming, then compute sup_pi Psi_G directly. Include the known stress tests: deterministic-vs-randomized two-world example, negative regret under adaptive known-law play, grid/continuum mismatch, and hidden-terminal atoms.","title":"Finite LP stress tests","why_promising":"Small exact games can quickly catch sign errors, missing randomization, bad comparator conventions, or incorrect Bayes-envelope formulas before explorers invest in an abstract proof."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"Previous iid block-splicing failed because the best fixed comparator is based on expected total reward, not realized per-block optima. Multi-scale constructions may again collapse to a Bayes-average quote unless quote levels, weights, and hidden types are arranged very carefully.","suggested_lenses":["lower-bound construction","Bayes-gap computation","counterexample search"],"summary":"Use the finite-grid identity as a design tool for multi-scale lower bounds under a tail envelope B(h). Construct finite classes with hidden delayed value at several delay scales, compute Psi_G for candidate priors, and test whether the lower bound can approach the truncation upper rate T^((alpha+1)/(2alpha+1)) for B(h)=h^-alpha.","title":"Multi-scale Bayes-gap lower rates","why_promising":"The one-block qD atom is known to be too weak. The exact Bayes-gap value may reveal how to accumulate indistinguishable delayed value across scales without the fixed-comparator collapse."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Approximate indistinguishability bounds can accidentally upper-bound the wrong posterior envelope when reward curves depend on histories. KL/TV control may be too weak to preserve separated fixed-comparator values over enough rounds.","suggested_lenses":["Le Cam/Fano lower bound","information theory","proof strategy"],"summary":"Develop limited-leakage versions of the hidden-terminal lower bound where worlds are not identical-prefix but remain hard to distinguish for many decisions. Formalize a per-round Bayes reward upper bound using TV or KL distance between decision-history laws, then search for constructions with repeated small information leaks and valuable delayed rewards.","title":"Limited-leakage lower bounds","why_promising":"This is a plausible route to matching rates: instead of a single terminal reveal that teaches the world, keep posterior uncertainty alive while regret accumulates."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"The obstruction may disappear under a doubling-style horizon-free policy or when regret is measured only at all sufficiently large horizons. The deterministic block parameters must be chosen so earlier blocks do not dominate or let the learner infer all future deadlines too easily.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","model taxonomy","proof strategy"],"summary":"Formalize the candidate anytime known-law obstruction using super-growing deterministic blocks with two possible deadline-relevant prices. Prove or refute that a single horizon-free policy for a known infinite exogenous sequence must incur linear regret along some horizon subsequence, despite every fixed finite horizon being oracle-learnable.","title":"Anytime known-law boundary","why_promising":"The solver's characterization is horizon-aware. This direction clarifies whether the final theorem needs a separate regime for horizon-free learnability, which the original problem's quantifiers may require."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"If the intended model is explicitly exogenous, this direction is only a scope note. If action dependence is allowed, the original regret definition itself may need causal counterfactual semantics before any theorem can be stated.","suggested_lenses":["model audit","counterexample search"],"summary":"Audit whether the market protocol permits P_t to depend on the learner's past quotes. If yes, define comparator paths and counterfactual rewards precisely, then test whether the delayed full-information upper reduction and finite-grid Bayes identity fail through simple adaptive-price examples.","title":"Action-dependent market audit","why_promising":"All accepted upper and Bayes-value arguments rely on public exogenous prices. This direction prevents the final theorem from silently proving a result for the wrong protocol."}],"solution_status":"maybe"}
